Energy · October 2, 2026
G7 will release 100 million barrels over four months and does not say how much is beyond its March commitments
The leaders' statement promises a substantial diesel release in the first 20 days and gives no number for it. Of the 400 million barrels International Energy Agency members pledged in March, about 325 million had been released.
100 million barrels coordinated release beginning immediately
4 months the period named for that release
20 days days named for the diesel release
400 million barrels March coordinated release
What happened
On October 2, 2026, the leaders of the G7 countries agreed to release 100 million barrels of oil over four months, coordinated through the International Energy Agency, to begin immediately. Their statement promises a substantial diesel release within the first 20 days and gives no number for it. It sets that release "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled."
Weekly U.S. retail diesel was 6.382 dollars a gallon on September 28. On March 2 it was 3.897. The Independent says prices have rocketed in recent months because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's war in Ukraine. The G7 says the measures are meant to "shield households and businesses from price shocks", and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage.
The European Commission's Energy Union task force said EU diesel supply remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high.
The French presidency's readout puts the figure as a ceiling: up to 100 million barrels within four months, notably of diesel and crude oil.
In March, International Energy Agency members pledged 400 million barrels; by October 2 around 325 million of that March action had been released. The October statement does not say how much of its release is beyond those commitments. On September 30 President Donald Trump was still "thinking about" banning diesel exports. On October 1 the German government said buyers had drawn a first part of the release it authorized on March 18, and that the market did not consider a further release necessary. On October 2, hours after the agreement, he said he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, and the joint statement commits the seven countries to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between themselves.
The International Energy Agency said in March that the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products. A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells. The October decision also says the countries will coordinate maintenance schedules across their refineries to prevent simultaneous shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization where feasible, and it encourages engagement with countries that hold significant refining capacity to boost production of refined products, particularly diesel. The agency's September report says net exports of diesel and gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390,000 barrels a day in August, just over a quarter of levels before the war in the Middle East, and that exports from the Gulf and Russia together were 1.6 million barrels a day lower than in February. On September 30, Energy Secretary Chris Wright said diesel supplies are "tight" because conflicts involving Russia and the Middle East have cut exports.
The catch
A massive amount of diesel, and no number for it
President Donald Trump posted that Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil and that the process will begin immediately. Reuters reported that the United States had demanded 100 million barrels of diesel from large European countries within 20 days, and that EU governments that day discussed a French proposal for 50 million barrels of diesel from Europe and 50 million barrels of crude oil from International Energy Agency members. The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks". The joint statement gives no diesel number, names no country that will supply it, and reports nothing delivered.
Donald Trump · October 2, 2026 · Truth Social, carried by Trump's Truth
Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately.
