The export ban
H.R. 10422, introduced September 16, would bar diesel exports after fourteen consecutive days above $5 a gallon, and "was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs".
European Commission spokeswoman Anna-Kaisa Itkonen said, "We fully reject any ban on diesel."
Ursula von der Leyen, European Commission president · October 2, 2026 · X post, via RTÉ
We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks.
RTÉ
What comes next
The G7 says it will meet again through the International Energy Agency in the coming days to discuss more diesel releases if needed. It invites the agency to monitor the impact of the measures and whether the commitments are carried out, and it says a follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days.
On September 29, before the G7 decision, the U.S. Energy Department offered an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, continuing the 172 million barrels the United States announced in March. Participating companies return the borrowed barrels later with extra barrels. Bids are due October 6. Five previous solicitations awarded more than 133 million barrels across four completed exchanges, and deliveries under the four exchanges are scheduled for November and December.
What we do not know yet
The G7 statement, the French readout and the International Energy Agency's account of the meeting do not say how much of the 100 million barrels is beyond the March pledge, how it divides between diesel and crude, which countries will supply it, or whether any of it has been delivered. The prices are from before the decision: the newest weekly diesel reading is September 28 and the newest Brent reading is September 29.
Source history
“100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months” France 24 reported on October 2; NBC News reported on October 2; UK Government reported on October 2
